.SH THE CUTOVER RAN 2026\-08\-30, AND WHAT IT BOUGHT
.nf

  FLIP  backend         oracle -> wsl
  OK    long_readout    0 (was 4)      <- the wedge is GONE
  OK    soft_lockup     0 (was 1)
  OK    runners_active  12 (was 12), and online at GitHub
  HOLD  machine_id, hostname, accounts, crontabs, etc_selfdev, verbs_on_path
  mem_total_mb 5925 -> 7941
.fi
.PP
It cost `ssh monkey', which RESOLVED below fixes with a port. Two findings from
that week are durable and outlive the fix:
.PP
.B A KERNEL\-TUN tailscaled BREAKS dexter's own tailnet.
Its `ts\-input' chain lands in the SHARED netns carrying
`\-s 100.64.0.0/10 ! \-i tailscale0 \-j DROP', which drops tailnet traffic
arriving on dexter's own mirrored eth0. 2223 went dead the moment monkey booted
with systemd; `tailscale down' on monkey restored it instantly.
`\-\-netfilter\-mode=off' is NOT enough \- the tailscale0 interface is the
conflict. monkey is logged out now, so this only matters if anyone re\-enables it.
.PP
.B The witness certified identity FILES, not REACHABILITY.
It exited 0 printing HOLD ts_ip for a node that was DOWN when graded, reading
`tailscaled.state' off disk. Closed 2026\-08\-31 by `ssh_hostkey'.
.SS And monkey's root went back onto the drive that caused realisateur#274
Not a classification quirk \- measured on the Windows side 2026\-08\-30:
.nf

  D:  DiskNumber 1  BusType USB   WD Elements 2621          3726 GB
  C:  DiskNumber 0  BusType NVMe  KINGSTON OM8PGP4512Q-A0    477 GB
.fi
.PP
This is not a tradeoff nobody weighed. It is a DOCUMENTED FIX, REVERTED.
.PP
realisateur#274/#275, 2026\-08\-14: monkey.vdi was on D:, THIS drive, which
logged 1580 `disk' Event ID 11 controller errors between 08\-07 and 08\-14 \- one
every 5\-10 minutes for a week. One landed on an NTFS transaction\-log flush,
VirtualBox lost its handle, root remounted read\-only, and sshd then RESET EVERY
CONNECTION AT KEY EXCHANGE because it could not write. All 14 accounts were
unreachable for hours and nothing reported it. `bin/monkey\-vdi\-to\-internal.sh'
exists to undo exactly this, and its stated invariant is that it NEVER WRITES
TO D:.
.PP
`bin/monkey\-watch.sh' says so at the point of measurement:
.nf

  # If this ever reads EXTERNAL-USB again, someone reverted the fix and the
  # page should say so rather than waiting to be asked.
.fi
.PP
It read EXTERNAL\-USB because this runbook told the operator to put it there.
The instruction "Imports land on D:, never C:" was written to protect C: SPACE
and directly contradicts #275's invariant; the two were never reconciled. That
line is corrected above \- the tar is bulk scratch and belongs on D:, the guest
disk does not.
.PP
.B How bad is it RIGHT NOW: not an emergency, and still wrong.
Measured 2026\-08\-30 on the Windows event log:
.nf

  Event ID 11 on \eDevice\eHarddisk1\eDR2: 5 in the LAST 30 DAYS
    (the outage week ran ~1580 in seven days)
  newest 2026\-08\-28 04:47; ZERO since the import began 13:36 today
  ReadErrorsTotal 0, Temperature 42, PowerOnHours 5094
.fi
.PP
So the drive is emitting the same error class at about one per six days rather
than 225 per day. It is not failing now. It IS the drive that failed, and #274
escalated from occasional to catastrophic, so this is a placement to correct
deliberately rather than an alarm to answer tonight.
.PP
.B monkey\-watch reported it correctly and immediately.
`vmhost_classify_disk' maps the drive LETTER (C: internal, D: EXTERNAL\-USB)
rather than probing the bus, so its EXTERNAL\-USB was an assumption \- but the
assumption is TRUE here, confirmed by `Get\-Disk'. The seam itself is working:
backend reads `wsl', disk_raw reads `D:\ewsl\-migration\emonkey'.
.PP
.B Moving it back is not free.
C: has 52G free while still holding the 37G rollback VDI. Landing the 23.32G
vhdx there leaves ~28G on a 477G system disk, about 6%. It fits, but it is
tight until the rollback VDI is released \- which cannot happen until the
cutover is ACCEPTED. So the order is: decide, then accept, then move; not
move, then decide.
.SH RESOLVED 2026\-08\-31: IT WAS A PORT, NOT A ROUTE
None of A, B or C was needed. monkey's sshd failed for ONE reason: it wanted
:22, which Windows sshd holds in the shared namespace. Every other distro there
answers on a nonstandard port \- 2223 IS dexter's Ubuntu \- so monkey takes 2224.
`ssh monkey' resolves again, `authorized_keys' still decides access, no tailnet
ACLs, and monkey stays OFF the tailnet. The host keys prove the PORT selects the
host, which is the hazard that started this:
.nf

  :2224  SHA256:kpgptBBMjKCzuaqVYyjRl+ibslHivDtMrixmJjgLmgc  root@monkey
  :2223  SHA256:ucszAvXSAnURsOFPN3iv64yaHzzApz/p3f2OdoVkef8  dexter's Ubuntu
.fi
.PP
The byte\-identical\-keys result was never a property of the tailnet. It is what
you get when NOTHING on monkey listens and the address falls through to whatever
else is in the namespace. Made permanent in `ssh.socket.d/override.conf' \- which
also took `is\-system\-running' from degraded to running, ssh.socket being the
unit that had FAILED. List BOTH families: a bare `ListenStream=2224' bound only
[::]:2224 and the route was refused. monkey then left the tailnet; its identity
was doing no work except making the witness pass.
.SS The witness now checks reachability, not identity files
ts_ip and ts_nodeid are gone. `ssh_hostkey' replaces them, measured from OUTSIDE
and graded MUST\-HOLD: it asserts the failure that happened \- the name landing on
dexter \- and cannot pass for a host nothing can reach. A `wsl:' target reads
ABSENT and exits 5 BY DESIGN; that is the rescue route, not the verdict.
`accepted.tsv' is the baseline now.
.SH ROLLBACK NEEDS BOTH DRIVES, AND THE DISK MOVE IS DEFERRED
etat.sh measures the 37.02G vdi on C: and reports rollback intact. That is half
the answer: the CONFIG and SAVED STATE are on D:, so the resume depends on the
USB drive. There are also TWO files named monkey.vdi \- the 15.35G D: copy is
registered and attached to nothing, residue from the #275 move.
.PP
monkey's ext4.vhdx is still 23.73G on D:. `wsl \-\-manage \-\-move' returns
ERROR_SHARING_VIOLATION, twice, the second time after a 30s settle: terminating
a DISTRO does not detach its disk, because the utility VM holds every registered
vhdx while ANY distro runs. It needs `wsl \-\-shutdown', which stops Ubuntu and
therefore Zach's channel. DEFERRED to the retirement window rather than spending
an outage now \- C: is 51G free today and ~90G once the vdi goes, monkey is
bounced there anyway, and the drive is quiet: 5 Event ID 11 in 30 days, zero
since the import. NO DATA MOVED in either attempt.
.SH 0. BASELINE, BEFORE ANYTHING TOUCHES MONKEY
.nf

  ./constate.sh > before.tsv && cat before.tsv
.fi
.SH 1. REHEARSAL \- A THROWAWAY COPY; MONKEY KEEPS RUNNING
Answers by measurement what no issue currently knows: whether systemd comes up,
whether the 12 runner units start, and whether tailscaled works inside a distro
under networkingMode=mirrored. Nothing here touches monkey. About 27G, 5 min.
.PP
ON dexter (route: ssh dexter):
.nf

  # the distros share ONE netns, so check for a collision BEFORE importing:
  # monkey listens on nothing hermes uses today, but 8643 and 8090 are taken.
  wsl.exe \-d hermes \-u root \-\- ss \-ltn | awk 'NR>1{print $4}' | sort \-u

  mkdir \-p /mnt/d/wsl\-migration
  ssh \-n \-i ~/.ssh/id_dexter_monkey zach@100.121.83.23 \e
\&    'sudo tar \-c \-\-numeric\-owner \-\-xattrs \-\-acls \-C / \e
\&       \-\-exclude=./proc \-\-exclude=./sys \-\-exclude=./dev \-\-exclude=./run \e
\&       \-\-exclude=./tmp \-\-exclude=./mnt \-\-exclude=./media \e
\&       \-\-exclude=./lost+found \-\-exclude=./swapfile \e
\&       \-\-exclude=./var/lib/tailscale .' \e
    > /mnt/d/wsl\-migration/monkey\-rehearsal.tar
.fi
.PP
.B The whole of /var/lib/tailscale is dropped ON PURPOSE here
so the rehearsal takes a FRESH identity and monkey's own node is never at risk.
Excluding just tailscaled.state is enough \- verified 2026\-08\-30, the identity
is in that file's `_machinekey' and `_profiles' keys, while profile\-data/ holds
only netmap caches \- but the whole directory is strictly safer and costs
nothing, because the rehearsal wants no tailscale state at all.
.PP
.B The cutover KEEPS it, and that is the point.
`tailscaled.state' is what carries node nyzii8E1C221CNTRL and the address
100.121.83.23. Everything that reaches monkey by name \- `Host monkey' in every
agent's ssh config, and 12 runner registrations \- resolves through it.
.PP
ON dexter, Windows route. The TAR lands on D: because it is bulk scratch.
.B The IMPORT MUST NOT: see #274 below \- a guest disk on D: is the outage.
.nf

  wsl.exe \-\-import monkey\-rehearsal D:\ewsl\-migration\emonkey\-rehearsal \e
    D:\ewsl\-migration\emonkey\-rehearsal.tar
  wsl.exe \-d monkey\-rehearsal \-u root \-\- /bin/true
.fi
.SS How the import behaves while it runs, measured 2026\-08\-30
Three things, and the third is a trap that invites exactly the wrong recovery.
.PP
.B It is slow, and it is slower than the tar.
The 22.22G tar took about an hour; the import runs at roughly 0.3\-0.6 G/min, so
budget an hour again. Do NOT wrap it in a short `timeout' \- a 15\-minute one
was too short here by a factor of four.
.PP
.B It survives the shell that launched it.
`wsl.exe' is a WINDOWS process reached through interop, not a child of your ssh
session. Killing the ssh, or the local script driving it, does not stop the
import: measured, the vhdx kept growing from 5.28G to 6.49G after its launching
process was killed. That is usually what you want, but it means you cannot
cancel an import by hanging up.
.PP
.B The distro does NOT appear in `wsl \-l \-q' until the import COMPLETES.
So absence from the list means "not finished", NOT "not running", and a reader
who reads it as failure and retries gets:
.nf

  An install, uninstall, or conversion is in progress for this distribution.
  Error code: Wsl/Service/RegisterDistro/0x8000000d
.fi
.PP
which is WSL correctly refusing a second import, not a new fault. Watch the
vhdx SIZE to see progress, and the distro LIST to see completion:
.nf

  ls \-l /mnt/d/wsl\-migration/monkey\-rehearsal/ext4.vhdx   # progress
  wsl.exe \-l \-q | grep \-x monkey\-rehearsal                # completion
.fi
.PP
A stale ext4.vhdx from a killed import cannot be deleted from the WSL side
(`Permission denied' on drvfs while Windows holds it); clear it from the
Windows route, or leave the original import to finish.
.SS The first boot is the safe window, and it is the only one
monkey carries no /etc/wsl.conf \- it has never been a distro \- so the FIRST
.B wsl \-d
boots WITHOUT systemd, because systemd=true is off by default. Nothing of
monkey's starts. That is the one window in which to disarm what must not run,
and it closes the moment section 2 writes a wsl.conf.
.PP
.B MASK THE RUNNERS BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE, OR THE REHEARSAL ATTACKS THE FLEET.
monkey has 12 enabled actions.runner units holding registered agent names
(monkey\-senechal, agentId 21, and eleven more). Under systemd they would
connect to GitHub as the SAME agents as the live monkey and contend for its
jobs. The rehearsal is meant to cost nothing; this is how it would cost the
fleet instead.
.nf

.B systemctl mask DOES NOT WORK ON THE RUNNER UNITS.
Measured 2026\-08\-30: they are regular files in /etc/systemd/system, which mask
refuses to overwrite \- `mask' reported success and left all 12 .wants symlinks
in place, so all 12 would still have started. Disable them and delete the
symlinks, then CHECK the count is zero:
.nf

  systemctl list\-unit\-files \-\-no\-legend | grep '^actions\\.runner\\.' \e
    | awk '{print $1}' | while read \-r u; do systemctl disable "$u"; done
  rm \-f /etc/systemd/system/multi\-user.target.wants/actions.runner.*
  ls /etc/systemd/system/multi\-user.target.wants/ | grep \-c '^actions\\.runner\\.'
    -> must print 0 BEFORE systemd is ever started

  systemctl mask tailscaled cron ufw ssh tpm\-udev.path tpm\-udev.service
.fi
.PP
tailscaled and cron: the rehearsal must not reach the tailnet or fire monkey's
crontabs. ufw and ssh are the SHARED NETNS: ufw would install iptables rules
into the namespace hermes and Ubuntu live in, and ssh would bind :22 beside the
Windows sshd that is your control plane. tpm\-udev is the only thing that fails
on its own here \- WSL2 has no TPM device \- and masking it is what turns
systemd's state from `degraded' into `running'.
.PP
Only then apply section 2's fixes, restart, and record what actually happened:
.nf

  wsl.exe \-\-terminate monkey\-rehearsal
  wsl.exe \-d monkey\-rehearsal \-u root \-\- systemctl is\-system\-running
  wsl.exe \-d monkey\-rehearsal \-u root \-\- systemctl list\-units \-\-state=active
  wsl.exe \-d monkey\-rehearsal \-u root \-\- systemctl is\-enabled tailscaled
  wsl.exe \-d monkey\-rehearsal \-u root \-\- tailscale \-\-version
.fi
.PP
The tailscale question the rehearsal exists to answer is whether tailscaled can
run inside a distro at all under networkingMode=mirrored \- not whether it can
take monkey's identity. Test it with a FRESH auth key against a throwaway node
name, never with monkey's state. dexter's own tailscaled runs on WINDOWS,
OUTSIDE WSL, so it proves nothing about this.
.B The 2026\-08\-30 rehearsal answered half of it.
Run with \-\-tun=userspace\-networking (no kernel interface, no routes, so the
shared netns is untouched), tailscaled STARTED inside the distro and reached
`BackendState=NeedsLogin' \- a healthy daemon with no credentials. It created 0
kernel interfaces, so hermes was unaffected. /dev/net/tun is also present and
the binary is 1.102.1.
.PP
.B What is still open is the LOGIN half:
whether a logged-in tailscaled inside a distro coexists with dexter's Windows
tailscaled under mirrored networking. That needs a fresh auth key, which the
rehearsal deliberately did not mint. It is the last unmeasured thing in the plan.
.SS A rehearsal legitimately exits 5, and here is which fields say so
constate.sh grades against monkey's baseline, so the deliberate differences
show as failures. Expect exactly these, and nothing else:
.nf

  MOVED  hostname      monkey -> monkey\-rehearsal   (you named it that)
  ABSENT ts_ip, ts_nodeid                           (you excluded the state)
  LOST   runners_enabled, runners_active            (you disarmed them)
.fi
.PP
Everything else must HOLD, and on the 2026\-08\-30 run it did: machine_id
identical, accounts 18, crontabs 19, verbs 19, /etc/selfdev 4. On the real
cutover those three groups hold too and the exit is 0.
.PP
Write the answers to rehearsal\-verdict.tsv, then tear it down:
.nf

  wsl.exe \-\-unregister monkey\-rehearsal      # from PORT 22, see below
  rm \-f /mnt/d/wsl\-migration/monkey\-rehearsal.tar
.fi
.PP
.B Tear down from the Windows route, not from a distro.
Measured 2026\-08\-30: immediately after `wsl \-\-terminate', further wsl.exe calls
from inside Ubuntu failed with `cannot execute binary file: Exec format error'.
That is WSL's interop binfmt handler, not a broken wsl.exe, and it strands a
23G distro registered if it is the only route you have. Port 22 does not use
binfmt and was unaffected. Repair the distro-side handler with:
.nf

  sudo systemctl restart systemd\-binfmt
.fi
.PP
which is the same fixup dexter\-liveness.sh already runs before its interop
block. Unregister reclaims the vhdx: D: went straight back to 2.6T free.
.SH 2. GUEST PREP \- WHAT MAKES AN IMPORTED ROOT ACTUALLY BOOT
Apply INSIDE the imported distro, never on the live guest, so a failed import
costs nothing.
.nf

  # / is mounted by UUID and no such device exists in WSL2; /swapfile likewise
  sed \-i 's|^/dev/disk/by\-uuid|#&|; s|^/swapfile|#&|' /etc/fstab

  # WSL2 writes /etc/resolv.conf and owns the interface; these fight it for DNS
  systemctl mask systemd\-networkd systemd\-resolved multipathd lvm2\-monitor \e
    ModemManager

  cat > /etc/wsl.conf <<'EOF'
  [boot]
  systemd=true
  [user]
  default=zach
  [network]
  hostname=monkey
  EOF
.fi
.PP
hostname=monkey matters: Ubuntu and hermes BOTH answer `dexter', and a third
distro answering `dexter' makes every host\-keyed probe ambiguous.
.SH 3. CUTOVER
Only after the rehearsal has a recorded verdict.
.nf

  ssh monkey 'sudo systemctl stop "actions.runner.*"'   # quiesce, do NOT deregister
  ./constate.sh > before\-cutover.tsv
.fi
.PP
ON dexter. The same tar as section 1 but KEEPING tailscaled.state \- that state
is what makes `Host monkey' and 12 runner registrations keep resolving.
.nf

  ssh \-n \-i ~/.ssh/id_dexter_monkey zach@100.121.83.23 \e
\&    'sudo tar \-c \-\-numeric\-owner \-\-xattrs \-\-acls \-C / \e
\&       \-\-exclude=./proc \-\-exclude=./sys \-\-exclude=./dev \-\-exclude=./run \e
\&       \-\-exclude=./tmp \-\-exclude=./mnt \-\-exclude=./media \e
\&       \-\-exclude=./lost+found \-\-exclude=./swapfile .' \e
    > /mnt/d/wsl\-migration/monkey.tar
.fi
.PP
Windows route. The VM is SAVED, never deleted \- that is the rollback.
.nf

  & 'C:\eProgram Files\eOracle\eVirtualBox\eVBoxManage.exe' controlvm monkey savestate
  wsl.exe \-\-import monkey D:\ewsl\-migration\emonkey D:\ewsl\-migration\emonkey.tar
  wsl.exe \-d monkey \-u root \-\- /bin/true
.fi
.PP
Apply section 2 inside it, then verify BEFORE trusting it:
.nf

  ./constate.sh \-\-target wsl:monkey \-\-diff before.tsv
.fi
.PP
Every MUST\-HOLD field must hold and backend must FLIP oracle \-> wsl. A MOVED
machine_id or an ABSENT ts_nodeid means STOP and roll back.
.SH ROLLBACK
Reversible at every step because the VDI is never touched.
.nf

  wsl.exe \-\-terminate monkey
  wsl.exe \-\-unregister monkey
  & 'C:\eProgram Files\eOracle\eVirtualBox\eVBoxManage.exe' startvm monkey \-\-type headless
  ./constate.sh \-\-diff before.tsv
.fi
.PP
Expect backend back to oracle and every field held.
.SH THE TWO GATES ONLY ZACH CAN PASS
Neither blocks the rehearsal. The second blocks only runner re\-registration,
and selfdev\-runner\-provision.sh retries daily, so it is recoverable after the
fact rather than a stop.
.nf

HUMAN\-STEP
  what:     approve the forced verb\-build cut so fixed host tools reach dexter
  where:    github.com/hf7y/verbs \-> Actions \-> build\-verbs \-> the waiting run
